# 24. Agent Program Rules

## Agents Shell

Agents Shell is a closed baseline.

It includes:

- Runtime/preference status
- WebSocket state
- Search/navigation
- Agent cards
- Permission UX
- Shared panel shell
- Loading/empty/error
- Responsive behavior
- Dark theme
- i18n
- RTL and Persian typography

## Agent-by-Agent Completion

Agent business logic should normally be completed one Agent at a time.

Shared provider or runtime foundations may serve multiple Agents, but they do
not authorize simultaneous redesign of multiple Agent business panels.

For every Agent outcome:

1. RCA
2. Dependency audit
3. Source-of-Truth audit
4. Backend/API audit
5. Database/cache audit
6. Security audit
7. Scheduler/worker audit where applicable
8. Provider audit
9. Data correctness
10. Frontend functionality
11. Professional redesign
12. Performance verification
13. Browser QA
14. Human QA
15. Regression
16. Build/deploy
17. Git closeout
18. Freeze

## Agent Card Standard

Every Agent card should communicate:

- Agent name
- Short purpose
- Operational status
- Last run or Never run
- Execution class
- Meaningful result count
- Accuracy only when calculable
- Dry Run status
- Blocked reason where applicable
- Clear action such as Open Agent

Do not show:

- Raw keys
- Fake values
- Misleading zeroes
- Undefined/null
- Excessive capabilities
- Contradictory status

## Agent Control Panel

Use the shared shell.

Show:

- Name
- Purpose
- Operational status
- Effective mode
- Side-effect classification
- Last run
- Primary action
- Close action

Business tabs remain Agent-specific.

Agent Settings must not duplicate:

- Global settings
- Credentials
- Provider Connections
- Runtime mode
- Kill Switch
- Shared risk settings

Agent Integrations should show read-only status and link to the canonical
owner.

## Agent Data Integrity

Every metric must have a definition and canonical source.

Card, panel, overview, results, history, database and API must agree.

Unknown values display `N/A`, not fake zeroes.

Agent completion does not require a fixed number or fixed names of business
tabs.

The final information architecture must follow the Agent's specialized user
needs and real data capabilities.

The shared Agent Product Template standardizes shell behavior, not business
content.

Every Agent outcome must document:

- Why each section exists
- What user question it answers
- Its canonical data owner
- Its unavailable behavior
- Its relationship to other sections

Decorative, duplicate or unsupported sections must not be created merely for
visual symmetry.

## Artemis Compatibility Requirement

Every Agent is both:

1. A specialized user-facing product, and
2. A potential machine-consumable evidence producer, control authority,
   feasibility authority, allocation authority or execution component for
   Artemis.

Agent product completion must therefore assess two separate dimensions:

- USER PRODUCT COMPLETENESS
- ARTEMIS INTEGRATION READINESS

The user-facing UI must not be treated as the Agent's only consumer.

Every Agent outcome must identify:

- Its verified role in the Artemis decision chain
- The questions it answers
- The markets, symbols and timeframes it covers
- The canonical data owner
- The canonical machine-readable output
- Freshness and data-quality semantics
- Supporting and conflicting evidence
- Limitations
- Authority boundaries
- Whether its output is advisory, vetoing, sizing, feasibility or execution
- Current Artemis integration-readiness state

Do not retrofit a different ad hoc Artemis payload for each Agent.

Until the shared canonical Artemis contract is approved and frozen, an Agent
may expose a scoped adapter proposal, but it must not create a parallel shared
contract owner.

Agent completion does not mean automated-trading readiness.

No Agent may claim that it is connected to Artemis, decision-eligible or
execution-eligible without verified end-to-end evidence.

